[Unit]
Description=Synthetic Enterprise worker tick timer — fire the continuity check every 60s (the wake backstop + the origin-staged-doc path). The 60s cadence is the worst-case wake latency for a staged doc; worker-tick.path wakes faster on a LOCAL staged doc, this timer additionally catches ORIGIN-staged docs (its tick runs _sync_origin_staging) and spontaneous below-target work. Independent of the .path trigger: R15 — kill the .path unit and this still wakes on the next tick.

[Timer]
# First check 30s after boot (let daemons settle). A continuity timer that is not armed is
# fail-silent (R15) — arming it is the whole point. Persistent replays a missed tick after a
# sleep/downtime so a resumed machine wakes promptly.
OnBootSec=30s
#
# ── WEEKEND BUDGET CADENCE, 2026-08-22 (director: 94% of the weekly allowance used, resets Monday
#    2026-08-24; "conserving is now the priority over shipping") ──────────────────────────────────
# 60s was never the observed cadence. Because this is OnUnitActiveSec (measured from the END of the
# last activation) and a tick runs 15-30 min, the real spacing was tick-duration + 60s. MEASURED,
# not assumed: `journalctl --user -u worker-tick.service --since "24 hours ago" | grep -c Starting`
# = 65 starts in the 24h to 2026-08-22 06:45Z, and `model_tier_log.jsonl` records 66 SPAWNED
# claude-opus-5 invocations over the same window with no other caller — so the tick IS the model
# spend, one Opus invocation per start, and its prompt carries the whole ~130-item unprocessed
# staging list every time.
#
# 4h was chosen against the ONE thing that must not break this weekend: publishing. Publishing does
# not run through here — `sim_runner.py` calls `background/process_run_complete.py` directly and
# contains no model reference at all (grep: 0 hits for claude/claude_bin), so slowing this timer
# cannot slow or wedge a publish cycle. What the tick still buys at 4h is a periodic autonomous
# look at a publisher that has broken before (the 28h outage to 2026-08-22 04:44Z), which is why
# this is slowed and NOT stopped: the kill switch (`.build_executor_enabled`) would also take that
# away, and it is director-reserved besides.
#
# ── MONDAY 2026-08-24: REVERTED, BUT NOT TO 60s ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The stand-down's revert fell due today and the director handed the cadence over with it. Going
# straight back to 60s was refused on the weekend's own measurement, and the reasoning is worth
# more than the number.
#
# THE WEEKEND WAS AN EXPERIMENT NOBODY DESIGNED, AND IT RETURNED A RESULT. Cutting tick spawns ~90%
# (66/day to ~6/day) cost nothing measurable: publishing ran untouched, and the work not done was
# investigation that moved no level. Read against the fortnight either side, that is not a story
# about the weekend — it is a story about what the ticks were choosing. Level moves, from
# `gate_authorizations.jsonl`: 2026-08-19 was the last day any level moved AT ALL. 08-20 and 08-21
# ran the FULL 60s cadence, ~66 Opus invocations each, and recorded ZERO. So the collapse predates
# the stand-down by two full-rate days, and rate cannot be its cause.
#
# WHAT THE TICKS WERE DOING INSTEAD, measured the same morning: `EP6_wall_protocol_typing` had taken
# 55 build passes since its level last moved and `SITE2_two_sided_wall_exhibit` 18, both invisible
# to a pass ceiling that was wired only to the idle tier. `H_harness` held 135 of the map's 316
# atoms. Meanwhile PB1/PB2/PB3 — the director's own 2026-08-11 ruling, and the only reader-visible
# work in the queue — sat `loop_stage: idle`, structurally undrawable for BUILD, for thirteen days.
#
# 30 MINUTES, AND WHY A MIDDLE RATHER THAN EITHER END. The draw changed this morning (build atoms
# now hit a ceiling; nineteen self-minted harness atoms retired; the PB atoms promoted to build).
# Restoring 60s the same hour would spend a full week's allowance proving a draw that has not run
# once. Staying at 4h would leave the change unmeasured. At OnUnitActiveSec — spacing is
# tick-duration + this value, and a tick runs 15-30 min — 30 min gives roughly 20-30 ticks/day
# against the fortnight's 66.
#
# THE TEST, so this is not a number someone has to re-litigate from taste: if the changed draw
# produces level moves and reader-visible landings at this rate, restore 60s. If it does not, the
# rate was never the constraint and 60s would only have bought more of the fortnight. Either
# outcome is an answer; the current number is the one that lets the question be asked.
OnUnitActiveSec=1800s
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
